# Cultural Report for First Day - Union 1st Corps Battlefield (Auwaerter, Olmsted Center for Landscape Preservation, 2021)
# Part A: Site History to 1863 (PDF pp. 35-68): the landscape of the First Day field before and just after the battle.
# Text variant: content-stream order (default_variant raw). '#sN' = sentence N of the page as numbered by `kwic.py page`.
# The report's own basis for the farm descriptions: the 1860 census (agricultural schedule), the damage claims filed after the
# battle (NARA RG 92 and the Pennsylvania state claims), the 1858 Hopkins map, the 1868-69 Warren map and the park's
# "1863 property research" (Kathy Georg Harrison); see its endnotes 31-131 on PDF pp. 69-72.
@ NPS-CLR-1STDAY
%basis 1860 census; damage claims (NARA RG 92 and Pennsylvania state claims); 1858 Hopkins and 1868-69 Warren maps; park 1863 property research (named in the report's endnotes, PDF pp. 69-72)

# --- the landscape as a whole ---
35 | general | First Day field: fenced fields, woodlots, three roads | - | 1863-07 | stated | The landscape northwest of Gettysburg fought over by the 1st Corps on July 1 was an orthogonal pattern of fenced fields with widely scattered woodlots and three main roads | The landscape was characterized by an orthogonal pattern >>> Chambersburg Pike, and Hagerstown Road
35 | woodlots | Willoughby Run banks | - | 1863-07 | stated | Willoughby Run had rocky banks lined by woods, willow clumps and briars | Through the center of this rolling landscape was Willoughby Run >>> willow clumps, and briars.
35 | woodlots | Town outlots used as woodlots or pasture | - | pre-1863 | stated | Town outlots of two to thirteen acres, cut from farms on the edge of Gettysburg in the early 19th century, were meant for village residents to use as woodlots or pasture | #s5
35 | fields | Farm composition on the First Day field | - | pre-1863 | stated | The farms had fenced fields, orchards, woodlots and a farmstead of house, barns and other outbuildings | #s6
39 | roads | Chambersburg Pike surface | RD-chambersburg-pike | 1812 | stated | The Chambersburg Pike was a macadam road completed through Cumberland Township in 1812 with a fifty-foot right-of-way | #s2
39 | roads | Unfinished railroad grade north of the pike | P-12 | 1838-1863 | stated | A short distance north of Chambersburg Pike lay the grade of an unfinished railroad running parallel to the turnpike toward Gettysburg | A short distance north of Chambersburg Pike was the grade of an unfinished railroad >>> on the approach to Gettysburg
39 | roads | Unfinished railroad grade: 1838 cuts through the ridges | P-12 | 1838-1863 | stated | The rail bed was built through the 1st Corps field in 1838 and needed cuts through the many ridges to give a level grade | Construction of the rail bed through the 1st Corps battlefield in 1838 required cuts >>> create a level grade
40 | roads | Unfinished railroad: no track laid before the war | P-12 | 1839-1863 | stated | Construction stopped in 1839 before track was laid, no track was laid west of Gettysburg before the Civil War, and local residents used the unfinished grade as a toll-free alternative to the pike | Construction stopped in 1839 prior to laying of tracks >>> alternative to Chambersburg Pike.

# --- Seminary campus, Schmucker farm, Thompson and Dustman lots ---
41 | fences | Seminary campus yards enclosed by paling fences | bld-schmucker-hall | 1830s-1863 | stated | The seminary's three main buildings were each set in a rectangular yard, enclosed and connected by paling fences | The grounds of the seminary campus were initially developed >>> paling fences (see fig. 1.15).
41 | fields | Seminary fields east of the buildings | bld-schmucker-hall | 1860-1863 | stated | The open sloping fields east of the seminary buildings were farmed in the early 1860s by the seminary steward Emanuel Ziegler, who grew wheat, oats, corn and potatoes on about eight and a half acres and cut the meadows for hay | By the early 1860s, Ziegler was cultivating wheat >>> cut the meadows for hay.
41 | fields | Schmucker farm (68 acres) | bld-schmucker-hall | pre-1863 | stated | Schmucker's farm of four town outlots (68 acres) included three fenced fields and the southern part of Seminary Grove | Schmucker purchased four of the outlots >>> southern part of Seminary Grove.
41 | buildings | Schmucker farm buildings, orchard and crops | bld-schmucker-hall | 1860-1863 | stated | On the eve of the war the tenant John T. Slentz Jr. worked the Schmucker farm: two-story frame tenant house, barn and sheds, carriage house and granary along the seminary lane, an apple orchard of about forty trees, and crops of corn, wheat, oats and hay | #s15-16
41 | fences | Fences between the Schmucker farm and the seminary campus | bld-schmucker-hall | 1860-1863 | hedged | Schmucker's farmland and the seminary campus were probably not strictly separated but were divided by fences along the seminary lane | Given the close relationship between Dr. Schmucker >>> fences along the seminary lane.
42 | fences | Mary Thompson house: arbor, garden, whitewashed picket fence | bld-thompson-house | 1860-1863 | stated | By the eve of the war Mary Thompson kept a vine-covered arbor and garden bed on the south side of her stone house, bordered by a whitewashed picket fence | She maintained a vine-covered arbor and garden bed >>> whitewashed picket fence.
42 | buildings | Mary Thompson house: barnyard and outbuildings | bld-thompson-house | 1860-1863 | stated | A small fenced barnyard with two small frame barns and a shed or privy bordered the Thompson house on the west | #s6
42 | fences | James Thompson house: whitewashed plank fence | bld-thompson-house | 1860-1863 | stated | James Thompson's smaller house across the pike had a whitewashed plank fence around its yard, which held a barn and shed along the seminary lane | Her son, James Thompson, lived with his wife and child >>> along the seminary lane.
42 | orchards | Dustman orchard lot south of the pike | bld-thompson-house | 1858 | stated | In 1858 Casper Dustman lived in a small brick house on a four-acre lot south of the pike and kept a large orchard of apple or peach trees there | Here in 1858, Dustman lived in a small brick house >>> apple or peach trees.
43 | orchards | Dustman fruit trees east of his house | bld-thompson-house | 1858-1863 | stated | At Dustman's new house (built about 1858) there was a large bank barn to the rear and, in the field to the east, fruit trees he had planted | #s2

# --- McPherson Ridge farms: Herbst, McPherson ---
43 | buildings | Herbst farmstead buildings | bld-herbst | 1857-1863 | stated | The Herbst farmstead on Hereter's Mill Road had a large bank barn on a stone foundation, a two-story log house with white painted siding, a wagon shed, corn crib, carriage house and stone spring house | #s13
44 | fields | Herbst farm: improved land, fenced fields, woodlot | bld-herbst,GZ-027 | 1860 | stated | By 1860 John Herbst managed 140 acres of improved land in nine fenced fields and twenty acres of unimproved wooded land (Herbst Woods at the northwest corner of the farm next to the McPherson farm) | By 1860, John Herbst was managing 140 acres of improved land >>> adjacent to the McPherson Farm.
44 | fields | Herbst farm: orchard and crops | bld-herbst | 1860 | stated | Herbst kept a large apple orchard north of the farmstead and raised hay, clover, wheat, corn, oats, potatoes and sweet potatoes | #s2
44 | buildings | McPherson barn: position and date | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1812 or earlier | hedged | McConaughy replaced an old log barn with the large bank barn (the existing McPherson barn); it may predate the 1812 turnpike, since it kept its unusual position in front of the house, toward the pike | McConaughy replaced the old log barn with a large bank barn >>> toward the turnpike.
45 | buildings | Old quarry pond fenced against livestock | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1860-1863 | stated | By the 1860s the old quarries on the McPherson farm were abandoned; the one near the farmstead was filled with water and surrounded by fencing to keep livestock out | By the 1860s, the quarries had been long abandoned. >>> to keep livestock out
45 | buildings | McPherson house, shed, wagon shed and bank barn in July 1863 | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | A photograph taken just after the battle (July 15, 1863) shows, right to left, a shed or privy, the stone-log farmhouse, a wagon shed and the bank barn; the house, little changed since it was built before 1798, faced east | The stone and log house (at right) built prior to 1798 >>> wagon shed, and bank barn.
46 | buildings | McPherson farm in the 1846 deed | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1846 | stated | The 1846 deed of sale for the McPherson farm lists a one-and-a-half-storey dwelling house, a bank barn, a wagon shed and other outbuildings, a well of water and an orchard of fruit trees | According to the deed of sale, John Bayard McPherson’s purchase included >>> an orchard of Fruit Trees
46 | fields | McPherson farm under tenant Slentz: fenced fields and crops | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1860-1863 | stated | By the early 1860s the McPherson farm worked by John T. Slentz spread across about twelve fenced fields, with wheat, corn, oats and grass, and a bank barn, wagon shed and corn crib | By the early 1860s, Slentz’s farm operation >>> and grass.
46 | orchards | McPherson farm orchard and cherry drive | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1860-1863 | stated | The orchard at the rear of the McPherson house had peach and apple trees, and a line of cherry trees stood along the entry drive from the turnpike | The orchard at the rear of the house had peach and apple trees >>> from the turnpike

# --- Oak Ridge farms: Forney, Wills, Gilbert, McClean ---
47 | fences | Stone fence on the Oak Ridge boundary of the Forney farm | FEN-seminary-ridge | 1859-1863 | stated | Along the eastern boundary of the Forney farm on Oak Ridge, on the later line of Doubleday Avenue, ran a stone fence with a jog that followed the old Mount Pleasant property line | Along the eastern property boundary on Oak Ridge >>> original Mount Pleasant property line.
47 | buildings | Forney (Fairview) farmstead | - | 1859-1863 | stated | The Forney farmstead on Mummasburg Road had a two-story frame house facing east with a two-story front porch, a log wing at the rear, a large frame barn to the south, outbuildings to the west (with a mechanic's shop), a well at the door and a spring near the barn | #s3-6
47 | fields | Forney farm crops | - | 1860-1863 | stated | By the early 1860s Forney kept dairy cows and grew fruit and crops that included wheat, oats, corn and timothy grass | #s12
47 | fields | Forney farm: eight fenced fields (first part of the sentence) | - | 1860-1863 | stated | Forney kept eight fenced fields, including prime cropland on the plateau south of the farmstead | He maintained eight fenced fields that included prime cropland on the plateau south of the
48 | orchards | Forney farm: pastures and market orchard on Oak Hill | - | 1860-1863 | hedged | Pastures on the sloping land of Oak Hill lay to either side of a large peach or apple orchard probably used for market production | farmstead, and pastures on the sloping land on Oak Hill >>> used for market production
48 | buildings | Wills barn rebuilt about 1860 | - | 1860 | stated | James J. Wills built a new frame barn about 1860 to replace one destroyed by a lightning fire, on the same site across from the farmhouse along the farm road from town | One of the first improvements that Wills made was to build a new frame barn >>> farm road from town (fig. 1.28).
48 | fields | Wills farm: seven fenced fields and crops | - | 1860-1863 | stated | By the early 1860s the Wills farm had seven large fenced fields, where William Job raised corn, wheat, oats and hay | By the early 1860s, the farm had seven large fenced fields. >>> corn, wheat, oats, and hay.
49 | orchards | Gilbert outlots: pasture and apple orchard | - | 1860-1863 | stated | By the eve of the war the two southern Gilbert outlots on Oak Ridge were pasture and the northern one next to the McClean farm held an apple orchard | #s2
49 | fences | Stone fence on the Forney boundary of the Gilbert outlots | - | 1860-1863 | stated | A stone fence along the Forney boundary formed the western edge of the Gilbert outlots | #s3
49 | woodlots | Harman farm woodlots | - | 1860-1863 | stated | Sizeable woodlots stood at the northwest corner of the Harman farm along Mummasburg Road and at the southeast corner on Oak Ridge | There were sizeable woodlots at the northwest corner of the farm >>> southeast corner on Oak Ridge.
49 | buildings | McClean farmstead | - | 1860-1863 | stated | The McClean farmstead had a log house built by 1822 with a later two-story brick addition, and a red-painted frame bank barn built about 1850 | The farmstead included a log house built by 1822 >>> built in ca. 1850
49 | orchards | McClean farm mixed fruit orchard | - | 1860-1863 | stated | West of the McClean farmstead on the slopes of Oak Ridge was a mixed fruit orchard that reached to the Forney boundary | #s9
49 | woodlots | Oak Hill summit: old field grown to thicket | GZ-027 | 1860-1863 | stated | North of the McClean orchard the summit of Oak Hill lay in an old field that had grown up into a thicket by the early 1860s; the only other land not in agriculture was a small grove on Oak Ridge south of Mummasburg Road | #s10-11
49 | fields | McClean farm crops | - | 1860 | stated | McClean's farm produced wheat, oats, corn, rye, Irish potatoes and hay | #s12
49 | orchards | Wills farm house orchard added after the battle | - | !after 1863 (post-battle addition) | stated | The orchard around the Wills farmhouse shown on the 1868-69 map was added after the battle | The orchard surrounding the house was added after the battle.

# --- Herr's Ridge: Harman, Herr, Spangler, Leaper, Crist, Wisler ---
50 | orchards | Harman farm orchard (1841 advertisement) | - | 1841 | stated | The 1841 advertisement listed a large orchard of prime engrafted apple trees (about 160-170) around the farmstead, with peach, cherry and plum trees | A large orchard of “prime engrafted apple trees >>> peach, cherry, and plum trees.
50 | buildings | Harman farm buildings (1841 advertisement) | - | 1841 | stated | The 1841 advertisement of the Harman farm named a pump and cistern, a two-story brick wash and smoke house, a double stone bank barn, a wagon shed and a corn crib | The 1841 advertisement also listed >>> and a corn crib
51 | buildings | Harman farm buildings (1862 advertisement) | - | 1862 | stated | The 1862 advertisement listed a twelve-room brick dwelling with kitchen, wash house, smoke house, stone bank barn with corn crib, wagon shed and other improvements, surrounded by the large orchard planted by the McCleans | The advertisement stated that the property contained most of the buildings >>> planted by the McCleans
51 | woodlots | Harman farm woodlot on the west side | - | 1862 | stated | The 1862 advertisement said the Harman farm had good timber, a reference to the woodlot along its west side | Harman also advertised that the farm had “good timber,” >>> along the west side.
51 | fields | Harman farm in 1863: tenant, crops, orchard | - | 1863 | stated | In 1863 the tenant William Comfort raised wheat, rye and cattle on the Harman farm and presumably managed the large apple orchard around the brick house | In 1863, the tenant was William Comfort >>> around the brick house
72 | orchards | Harman farm orchard: possibly post-battle | - | 1862-1869 | conflict | The report notes the orchard shown around the Harman brick house could be a post-battle addition because no damage claim was filed for it, against the 1862 advertisement that places an orchard around the house | The orchard could be a post-battle addition since it is shown >>> no claims for damage to it were filed.
53 | fields | Abraham Spangler farm in 1860 | - | 1860 | stated | In 1860 Abraham Spangler's farm had about forty improved acres north and south of the pike with pasture, an orchard and cultivated land growing wheat, oats, corn, potatoes and market produce | In 1860, Abraham Spangler’s farm contained a reported forty improved acres >>> market produce.
54 | orchards | Crist (Minnich) farm orchards | - | 1858 | stated | In 1858 the Minnich farm advertised three large orchards (west of the house across Herr's Ridge Road, a small fruit garden north of the house and a larger market orchard north of Willoughby Run) holding 1,000 peach trees, 100 apple trees and other fruit trees | Minnich maintained three large orchards on the property >>> and other fruit trees.
55 | fields | Wisler place: cornfield and potato patch | - | 1860-1863 | stated | The Wisler family kept a garden, a potato patch and a cornfield on their four acres by the early 1860s | The family also maintained a garden, a potato patch, and a cornfield on their four acres.

# --- the battle's effect on the landscape (breastworks, fences, damage claims) ---
57 | fences | Fences as obstacles and as breastwork material | E-01,E-18 | 1863-07 | stated | Troops tore down fences, trampled crops, used stone fences such as those on Oak Ridge as breastworks, and built new breastworks from stacked rails taken from the miles of wooden fences | As troops moved across the rural landscape, they trampled crops >>> and other debris.
57 | earthworks | Union rail breastworks no more than three feet high (Olmsted, July 1863) | E-01 | 1863-07 | stated | Frederick Law Olmsted, visiting two weeks after the battle, described the Union breastworks as not more than three feet high, like the slightest New England stone fence, with a tree trunk, fence rails, a broken wagon and sometimes a dead horse laid in | They are not more than three feet high >>> when it came handy
60 | earthworks | Gamble's right-angle rail breastwork on McPherson Ridge | FEN-gamble-cavalry | 1863-07-01 | stated | Some of Gamble's troops were entrenched behind piled-rail breastworks, such as the right-angle work near Calef's Battery that covered them from fire on Herr's Ridge | Some of the troops were entrenched behind piled-rail breastworks >>> Confederate fire on Herr’s Ridge.
62 | earthworks | Union artillery behind fence-rail breastworks south of the pike | E-01 | 1863-07-01 | stated | Late on July 1 Union artillery stood behind breastworks built of fence rails that extended south from the Chambersburg Pike | Union artillery, behind breastworks built of fence rails >>> against the Confederate advance.
62 | earthworks | Paul's brigade rail breastworks north of the seminary | E-01 | 1863-07-01 | stated | Regiments of Paul's brigade built breastworks of fence rails north of the seminary buildings | Regiments of Paul’s Brigade built breastworks out of fence rails north of the seminary buildings.
62 | earthworks | Baxter and Paul entrenched along stone walls on Oak Ridge | P-07 | 1863-07-01 | stated | Baxter's and Paul's brigades entrenched along the stone walls of the Forney farm boundary on Oak Ridge north to Mummasburg Road and added rail breastworks; the walls were overgrown and tumbled down | Both brigades entrenched along stone walls on Oak Ridge >>> built additional breastworks out of fence rails.
62 | fences | Overgrown, tumbled-down stone walls on the Forney boundary | P-07 | 1863-07-01 | stated | Baxter's and Paul's brigades lay in part behind the overgrown and tumbled-down stone walls along the east boundary of the Forney farm | Baxter’s and Paul’s brigades, hidden in part behind the overgrown >>> east boundary of the Forney Farm
63 | earthworks | Confederate rail breastworks built on July 4 along Seminary Ridge | E-18 | 1863-07-04 | stated | On July 4 Lee ordered breastworks built or strengthened along the whole ridge from Oak Hill south to Emmitsburg Road; through the seminary campus troops built stacked-rail breastworks east of the road and through Seminary Grove | After Lee and his staff departed headquarters >>> through Seminary Grove

# --- aftermath (July 1863) ---
65 | damage | Fences, houses and fields after the battle (Jacobs) | - | 1863-07 | stated | Professor Jacobs wrote that after the battle fences were swept away, houses shattered and the fields trodden down like a highway | Fences were swept away, houses shattered, and the fields trodden down like a highway.
66 | damage | Seminary fences destroyed; Schmucker's rail loss | bld-schmucker-hall | 1863-07 | stated | Schmucker reported the fences around the yard, garden and the whole Seminary lands nearly all levelled, and counted 3,108 rails lost from his farm | The fences around the yard and garden were nearly all leveled >>> rails lost from his adjacent farm
66 | damage | Seminary steward Ziegler lost most of his crops | bld-schmucker-hall | 1863-07 | stated | Emanuel Ziegler lost most of his crops of wheat, oats, corn and potatoes grown east of the seminary buildings | Emanuel Ziegler, the steward of the seminary campus, lost most of his crops >>> in the fields east of the buildings.
66 | hospitals | Seminary main building used as a hospital | H-03,bld-schmucker-hall | 1863-07 | stated | The seminary main building was used for weeks after the battle as a hospital for both sides, and those who died were buried on the campus | Union remains were scattered in the woods of Seminary Grove >>> buried on the campus grounds.
66 | hospitals | Dustman house used as a hospital; losses | bld-thompson-house | 1863-07 | stated | Casper Dustman's house suffered battle damage and was used as a hospital; he lost fences and four acres of wheat, straw and hay | North of the seminary campus, Casper H. Dustman’s house suffered battle damage >>> four acres of wheat, straw, and hay
66 | damage | Mary Thompson property damage | bld-thompson-house | 1863-07 | stated | Mary Thompson's property, used as Lee's headquarters, saw Confederate breastworks built, fences destroyed, a hog pen and stable damaged and two acres of corn and half an acre of grass lost | Mary Thompson, who purportedly remained in her stone house throughout the battle >>> half acre of grass.
66 | hospitals | McPherson farm buildings used to shelter the wounded | H-05,bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The McPherson farmhouse, bank barn, stables and wagon shed withstood the fighting and were used by the 1st Corps to shelter the wounded on July 1 and stayed filled with wounded under Confederate control | With its location near the center of fighting on July 1, the McPherson Farm suffered heavily >>> under Confederate control.
66 | hospitals | McPherson barn and wagon shed hospital, July 5 visitor's account | H-05,bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-05 | stated | A visitor on July 5 found the barn above and below, the wagon shed, tenant house, pig sty and open barnyard crowded with badly wounded men, with amputations done in the wagon shed on boards laid on trestles | On the morning of July 5, a visitor to the farm recalled >>> the work of amputation began
67 | damage | McPherson farm losses (tenant Slentz) | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | Slentz reported four tons of hay, 9 acres of wheat, 16 acres of corn, 4 acres of oats, 18 acres of grass and half an acre of potatoes lost | This included loss of livestock and crops including four tons of hay >>> a half acre of potatoes.
67 | fences | McPherson farm fence losses | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | Most of the fences along Chambersburg Pike were destroyed; Edward McPherson claimed 6,844 rails, 20 feet of boards and 138 posts | Most of the fences along Chambersburg Pike had been destroyed >>> 138 posts.
67 | damage | Dead horses burned with fence rails | - | 1863-07 | stated | Union troops cleaning up the battlefield used fence rails to burn dead horses | #s3
67 | woodlots | Herbst Woods: trees damaged but most standing | GZ-027 | 1863-07 | stated | In Herbst's woodlot, where Reynolds was mortally wounded, tree trunks were damaged by shell and shot but most trees remained standing with relatively little damage | To the south, John Herbst’s woodlot close to the McPherson farmstead >>> relatively little damage
67 | buildings | Herbst bank barn destroyed by fire in the battle | bld-herbst | 1863-07-01 | stated | Herbst claimed the loss of his large bank barn, destroyed by fire during the battle, perhaps about the time the nearby Harman barn was destroyed in the Confederate advance on the afternoon of July 1 | Although his farmstead was well to the south >>> afternoon of July 1.
67 | fences | Herbst fences torn down; rails burned | bld-herbst | 1863-07 | stated | Fences on the Herbst farm were torn down for troop and artillery movement; Herbst reassembled some but claimed 3,068 rails burned and 32 posts destroyed | Fences were torn down to allow movement of troops and artillery. >>> 32 fence posts destroyed.
67 | damage | Herbst crop losses | bld-herbst | 1863-07 | stated | Herbst claimed the loss of 26-30 acres of wheat and rye, 7 acres of oats and 11-15 acres of grass, with damage to oats, grass and 16 acres of corn | He also claimed the loss of 26-30 acres of wheat and rye >>> 16 acres of corn
67 | damage | Forney farm losses | - | 1863-07 | stated | The two armies burned 3,891 Forney fence rails, damaged the peach and apple trees and destroyed 32 acres of wheat, 30 of timothy, 10 of oats and 10 acres of the cornfield | The two armies had burned 3,891 of their fence rails >>> part of the cornfield.
67 | damage | McClean farm fences and crops | - | 1863-07 | stated | Moses McClean reported loss of much of his fencing and trampling of his hayfields and crops of oats, wheat and corn | Moses McClean reported loss of much of his fencing >>> wheat, and corn.
67 | damage | Wills farm losses | - | 1863-07 | stated | James Wills claimed the loss of 800 fence panels, 19 acres of corn, two 15-acre fields of wheat, 15 acres of oats and a field of grass | James Wills claimed the loss of 800 fence panels >>> and a field of grass.
68 | hospitals | Crist farmhouse used as a field hospital | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Michael Crist farmhouse on Herr's Ridge remained standing and was pressed into service as a field hospital | Both Herr’s Tavern and Crist farmhouse remained standing >>> field hospital.
68 | buildings | Harman barn and brick house burned | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The barn and brick house of the Harman farm, at or near the front line, were burned | In contrast, the Harman and Spangler farms to the east of the ridge >>> brick house on the Harman Farm.
68 | damage | Harman tenant losses | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Harman tenant William Comfort claimed the loss of 10 acres of wheat, 5 acres of rye and 11 cattle | William Comfort, the Harman tenant, claimed the loss of 10 acres of wheat >>> along with 11 cattle.
68 | hospitals | Abraham Spangler and Leaper buildings used as Confederate hospitals | - | 1863-07 | stated | The buildings on the Abraham Spangler and Isaac Leaper properties north of the pike were used as Confederate hospitals | The buildings on the Abraham Spangler and Isaac Leaper properties north of the turnpike >>> as Confederate hospitals.
68 | damage | Abraham Spangler's losses south of the pike | - | 1863-07 | stated | South of the turnpike Spangler's fields were damaged, with 20 bushels of wheat, 15 bushels of corn and 13 acres of pasture lost | South of the turnpike Spangler’s farm fields were damaged >>> 13 acres of pasture.
68 | fences | Leaper board fence destroyed | - | 1863-07 | stated | Isaac Leaper claimed destruction of 357 feet of board fence | Leaper also claimed damage to his garden >>> 357 feet of board fence.

