# --- Part B: analysis chapter and Landscape Features Inventory (PDF pp. 288-444): the 'At the time of the battle' histories of each feature ---
# These inventory entries describe the feature in 1863 in their History paragraph and the 2016-2019 condition in the following paragraphs;
# only the History sentence naming the battle is quoted here. The existing-conditions sentences are marked indirect or left out.
%basis the report's feature histories (Landscape Features Inventory, Tables 3.1-3.5); sources named in the report's endnotes (damage claims, 1860 census, Warren map, park research)
331 | general | First Day field in 1863: fenced fields, orchards, woodlots on the ridges | - | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 the 1st Corps battlefield was defined by a mostly orthogonal pattern of fenced fields and orchards, with woodlots on the ridges and on ground not suited to cultivation | In 1863, the historic character of the 1st Corps battlefield landscape was defined >>> not well suited to cultivation.
335 | general | Seminary Ridge in 1863 | bld-schmucker-hall | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 Seminary Ridge was anchored by the three seminary buildings that faced Gettysburg along the east side of a lane between Chambersburg Road and Hagerstown Road | In 1863, the battlefield landscape of Seminary Ridge on the 1st Corps battlefield was anchored >>> Hagerstown Road.
336 | general | McPherson Ridge in 1863 | bld-herbst,bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 McPherson Ridge was fenced farm fields from Hagerstown Road north beyond the unfinished railroad, with a large woodlot on the Herbst farm bordering the McPherson farm and Willoughby Run | In 1863, the battlefield landscape of McPherson Ridge consisted >>> Willoughby Run.
337 | woodlots | Open understory of the 1863 woodlots | GZ-027 | 1863-07 (open understory); naturalised since | stated | Naturalisation of woodlots has led to loss of the open understory that existed during the battle | #s8
338 | general | Oak Ridge in 1863 | - | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 Oak Ridge was fenced farm fields of the Wills and Forney farms from the unfinished railroad north to Oak Hill and from the crest of Oak Ridge west along a broad level plain to the foot of Herr's Ridge | In 1863, the battlefield landscape of Oak Ridge consisted >>> foot of Herr’s Ridge.
340 | general | Herr's Ridge in 1863 | - | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 Herr's Ridge was fenced farm fields across the valley between Willoughby Run and Herr's Ridge from Old Mill Road north to the unfinished railroad and along the ridge top north of the railroad | In 1863, the battlefield landscape of Herr’s Ridge consisted >>> north of the railroad.
348 | woodlots | Tree species of the 1863 battlefield | - | 1863-07 | stated | Trees in 1863 were primarily oak and hickory, with tuliptree, walnut, maple, ash and other native deciduous trees, found in woodlots, hedgerows and as specimens | Trees, primarily oak and hickory along with tuliptree >>> and as specimens.
349 | fences | Battle-era field fences: purposes and types | - | 1863-07 | stated | The small farm fields of the 1st Corps field were enclosed by a variety of fences to keep livestock in, protect crops or mark boundaries | The patchwork of small farm fields on the 1st Corp field >>> to mark property boundaries.
349 | fences | Battle-era field fences: Virginia worm and five-rail post-and-rail | - | 1863-07 | stated | Apart from stone fences, the field fences were wood rails in two main styles: Virginia worm, with rails 10-12 feet long laid in zig-zag with cross braces, and post-and-rail, typically five rails, which took up less area than worm fence | Aside from stone fences, these were typically of wood rails >>> less area than Virginia worm.
349 | fences | Field fences obstructed troops and were taken down for breastworks | E-01 | 1863-07 | stated | During the battle the field fences obstructed troop movements and were taken down to build breastworks | During the battle, these fences obstructed troop movements; they were also taken down to build breastworks.
349 | fences | Fences after the battle: rebuilt, then wire | - | 1863-1900 (after the battle) | stated | After the battle most fences were rebuilt, and by the end of the century cheaper wood-post and wire fences were introduced | After the battle, most of the fences were rebuilt, and by the end of the century >>> were introduced.
350 | fences | Reconstructed battle-period fences, 2009-2012 | - | 2009-2012 reconstruction (modern) | stated | The park began rebuilding battle-period fences on the 1st Corps field in 2009 and rebuilt 9,925 linear feet by 2012, with gaps where fences are known to have been dismantled during the battle | The park and its partners began reconstructing battle-period fences >>> reconstructed on the 1st Corps battlefield.
350 | fences | Reconstructed fences are not original material | - | 2016-2019 condition (modern) | stated | The existing battle-period fences define the obstacles soldiers met but their materials are non-historic, and about one quarter of the battle-period fences within the park have been rebuilt, the rest are missing | Evaluation: The existing battle-period fences on the 1st Corps battlefield contribute >>> the rest are missing.
288 | fences | Reconstructed battle-period fences of Virginia worm or post-and-rail type | - | 2016-2019 condition (modern, reconstructed) | stated | Battle-period wood fences found on the 1st Corps field today are mostly Virginia worm or post-and-rail types and delineate some but not all of the battle-period fields | Features found throughout 1st Corps battlefield include battle-period wood fences >>> battle-period fields
490 | fences | Park plan to rebuild all 1863 fences in the Major Battle Action Area | - | 1999 recommendation (modern) | stated | The 1999 General Management Plan recommended rebuilding all 1863 fences within the Major Battle Action Area, about 39 miles, because of their effect on the battle | The 1999 General Management Plan recommended that all 1863 fences >>> impact on the outcome of the battle.
490 | fences | Some 1863 fence lines need a second source | - | 2021 treatment guideline (modern) | stated | The report says a number of fences need a second source to document existence, type and alignment, for example north-south fences on the Forney and Harman farms, and should not be rebuilt without other evidence such as an orchard edge or road | There are a number of fences that need to have a second source >>> Examples include north-south fences on the Forney and Harman farms.
491 | fences | Fence lines documented but of unknown type | - | 2021 treatment guideline (modern) | stated | Where two sources document an 1863 fence line but not its type, the report suggests a simple three-rail post fence in the character of 1863 fences | Where there are two sources documenting a fence line in 1863 >>> character of 1863-style fences.
491 | fences | Five-rail post-and-rail fences of 1863 | - | 1863 (type stated in the 2021 treatment guidelines) | stated | The report refers to the five-rail post-and-rail fences of 1863 as the type that commercial three-rail fences resemble | Commerciallyavailable three-rail post-and-rail fences should not be used >>> five-rail post-andrail fences of 1863.
463 | fences | No conclusive documentation of the 1863 fence between Forney fields 5/6 and 7/8 | - | 1863-07 | stated | Drawing 3.2 notes inconclusive documentation of an 1863 fence between Forney fields 5/6 and 7/8 | Inconclusive documentation on 1863 fence between Forney fields 5/6 and 7/8
131/Drawing 1.3 | fences | Insufficient documentation for all fence lines and types, 1863-1895 | - | ~1863-1895 drawing note | stated | The report's 1863-1895 drawing notes that there is insufficient documentation to determine all fence lines and types | Insufficient documentation to determine all fence lines/types
299 | fences | Forney farm east boundary stone fence | P-07 | 1863-07 (modified after the battle) | stated | A battle-era stone fence, used as a breastwork and modified after the battle, extends along the east boundary of the Forney farm | A battle-era stone fence, used as a breastwork and modified after the battle >>> along the east boundary.
301 | fences | Gilbert outlots: three fields in 1863, stone fence on the west | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Gilbert outlots contained three fields in 1863 and were lined on the west side by a battle-era stone fence on the Forney property line | The outlots, which contained three fields in 1863 >>> on the Forney property line.
428 | fences | Oak Ridge dry-laid stone fence, tumbled down | P-07 | 1863-07 | stated | A dry-laid stone fence ran along the boundary of the Forney and Wills farms with the McClean farm and Gilbert outlots on both sides of Mummasburg Road | At the time of the battle, a dry-laid stone fence (wall) extended along the boundary >>> north and south of Mummasburg Road.
